  seconds: 1
  topic: Meeting Opening and Agenda Overview
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis, Board Chair, opened the meeting by outlining the agenda,
    which included presentations from three superintendent search firms. She explained
    the process for questions after each presentation and the board's plan for deliberation
    and potential decision-making.
- time: 00:02:02
  seconds: 122
  topic: Roll Call
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis conducted the roll call for the board members present.
    Daniel Feller, Claire Holman, Eleni Richardson, George Risch (late), Tyler Smith,
    and Dowling were present, while student representatives Kabisa and Davison were
    absent.
- time: 00:02:16
  seconds: 136
  topic: 'Presentation: Maine School Management Association (MSMA)'
  desc: Eric Waddell from MSMA presented his firm's services for superintendent searches.
    He highlighted MSMA's deep understanding of Maine's educational context, its responsiveness,
    and its comprehensive support for school boards and new superintendents, including
    a year-long orientation program.
- time: 00:17:09
  seconds: 1029
  topic: 'Q&A: Maine School Management Association'
  desc: Board members engaged Eric Waddell in a question-and-answer session following
    his presentation. Topics included the scope of post-hire services, MSMA's experience
    with Maine-specific challenges like school infrastructure and funding, and strategies
    for attracting diverse candidates.
- time: 00:17:13
  seconds: 1033
  topic: Post-Hire Services (Tyler Smith)
  desc: Tyler Smith inquired if MSMA's post-hire support, such as assigning a mentor
    and providing orientation, was included in the search service fee or required
    additional payment. Eric Waddell confirmed these were included as member services.
- time: 00:18:44
  seconds: 1124
  topic: Maine-Specific Challenges & Diversity (Eleni Richardson)
  desc: Eleni Richardson asked about MSMA's pulse on Maine's challenges, specifically
    mentioning school infrastructure, funding, and rising special education costs.
    She also pressed on how MSMA ensures a diverse candidate pool, given South Portland's
    unique demographics.
- time: 00:31:14
  seconds: 1874
  topic: Effectiveness Metrics (George Risch)
  desc: George Risch questioned MSMA's ability to measure the effectiveness of their
    placements, asking if they track superintendent retention or gather feedback on
    success. Eric Waddell admitted they don't formally track such data but noted the
    complexity of variables involved.
- time: 00:33:02
  seconds: 1982
  topic: Previous Search Involvement (Dowling)
  desc: Dowling asked if MSMA had conducted South Portland's last permanent superintendent
    search. Eric Waddell was unsure, noting his recent start and the timeframe of
    the previous hire, but offered to investigate.
- time: 00:34:34
  seconds: 2074
  topic: Equity & Diversity Approach (Rosemarie DeAngelis)
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis inquired about MSMA's approach to equity and diversity,
    particularly how they address these issues given the board's and MSMA's predominantly
    white composition. Eric Waddell explained they would work closely with the board
    to define and articulate these values in the district profile and candidate outreach.
- time: 00:36:46
  seconds: 2206
  topic: Constituent Outreach & Search Team Model (Rosemarie DeAngelis)
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis asked about MSMA's strategies for outreach to various
    constituent groups and their typical model for forming a search team. Eric Waddell
    stated they customize outreach based on board preferences and facilitate listening
    sessions with desired groups, emphasizing flexibility in team structure.
- time: 00:41:48
  seconds: 2508
  topic: MSMA's Weaknesses (Rosemarie DeAngelis)
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis asked Eric Waddell to identify MSMA's weak suit or areas
    for improvement. He cited capacity as a potential 'Achilles heel' due to having
    only two executives, but noted that early engagement in the search process helps
    manage this.
- time: 00:43:55
  seconds: 2635
  topic: Break
  desc: The board took a short break before the next presentation. Rosemarie DeAngelis
    ensured the next presenter, Dave Dusi, had his technology set up.
- time: 00:52:56
  seconds: 3176
  topic: 'Presentation: NSEC'
  desc: Dave Dusi, Executive Director of NSEC, introduced himself and his organization,
    highlighting his 33 years in education and NSEC's role in supporting districts
    across six New England states. He emphasized NSEC's collaborative and flexible
    approach to superintendent searches.
- time: 00:55:22
  seconds: 3322
  topic: NSEC Process Overview (Dave Dusi)
  desc: Dave Dusi detailed NSEC's search process, including broad candidate outreach,
    collaborative development of an application letter, and the creation of a candidate
    profile through focus groups and anonymous electronic surveys. He explained the
    role of a screening committee in reviewing applicants and recommending finalists
    to the board.
- time: 01:11:12
  seconds: 4272
  topic: 'Q&A: NSEC'
  desc: Board members questioned Dave Dusi on various aspects of NSEC's services.
    Discussions covered the number of active searches, NSEC's experience in Maine,
    and how the firm addresses diversity and manages public perception during a search.
- time: 01:12:07
  seconds: 4327
  topic: Current Searches (Dowling)
  desc: Dowling asked about the number of superintendent searches NSEC was currently
    conducting. Dave Dusi stated they had three active searches in Massachusetts and
    Connecticut, with proposals out for two more, including one in Maine.
- time: 01:13:05
  seconds: 4385
  topic: Diversity & Public Image (Eleni Richardson)
  desc: Eleni Richardson inquired about NSEC's data on diverse superintendent hires
    and how they manage the optics of a search, particularly given South Portland's
    recent public challenges. Dave Dusi explained that while they target diverse candidate
    sources, the final selection rests with the screening committee, and candidates
    are expected to research the district's history.
- time: 01:27:40
  seconds: 5260
  topic: Post-Selection Mentorship (Rosemarie DeAngelis)
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis asked about NSEC's post-selection mentorship and board
    relationship-building services. Dave Dusi explained NSEC offers entry plan work
    for new superintendents and is developing training for school boards on topics
    like governance and conflict resolution, which can be customized.
- time: 01:32:03
  seconds: 5523
  topic: Team Diversity & Board Involvement (Rosemarie DeAngelis)
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis questioned how NSEC ensures diverse perspectives within
    its team and how the full board is involved in the selection process. Dave Dusi
    noted his urban district background and willingness to connect with field experts,
    explaining that the board receives finalists after the screening committee's work.
- time: 01:42:47
  seconds: 6167
  topic: NSEC's Weaknesses (Rosemarie DeAngelis)
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis asked Dave Dusi to identify NSEC's weaknesses. He candidly
    stated that staffing capacity is their main challenge, as he is currently the
    primary consultant for Maine searches. He is actively working to recruit retired
    superintendents to expand their team.
- time: 01:46:13
  seconds: 6373
  topic: Break
  desc: The board took another short break before the final presentation. This allowed
    for a brief pause in proceedings.
- time: 01:50:07
  seconds: 6607
  topic: 'Presentation: Zeal Education Group'
  desc: Jeff Melendez from Zeal Education Group presented his firm's approach, emphasizing
    a 'concierge level' service and a process distinct from the other firms. He highlighted
    Zeal's diverse team and their focus on a deep understanding of the district's
    needs.
- time: 01:54:01
  seconds: 6841
  topic: Zeal Education Group Process Overview (Jeff Melendez)
  desc: Jeff Melendez detailed Zeal's process, which includes the board being the
    sole decision-maker in candidate selection, extensive proactive stakeholder engagement
    (including multilingual ThoughtExchange surveys), and the creation of a comprehensive
    leadership profile. He also introduced the concept of 'superintendent personas'
    to help the board identify the ideal leader.
- time: 02:13:58
  seconds: 8038
  topic: 'Q&A: Zeal Education Group'
  desc: Board members questioned Jeff Melendez on Zeal's unique methodology. Discussions
    focused on the development of superintendent personas, the board's direct involvement
    in candidate review, and the firm's strategies for broad and diverse community
    engagement.
- time: 02:13:58
  seconds: 8038
  topic: Superintendent Personas (Dowling)
  desc: Dowling asked about the origin and development of Zeal's six superintendent
    personas. Jeff Melendez explained they were developed by his team to help boards
    identify the specific type of leadership needed at a given time, emphasizing that
    no single candidate embodies all qualities.
- time: 02:16:28
  seconds: 8188
  topic: Board-Centric Selection (Tyler Smith)
  desc: Tyler Smith inquired further about Zeal's recommendation for the board to
    be the sole decision-maker in candidate selection, without a separate screening
    committee. Jeff Melendez argued that hiring the superintendent is a core board
    responsibility and crucial for building trust and consensus.
- time: 02:21:21
  seconds: 8481
  topic: Stakeholder Engagement (Eleni Richardson)
  desc: Eleni Richardson asked about Zeal's approach to stakeholder engagement, particularly
    for marginalized communities, beyond the ThoughtExchange tool. Jeff Melendez confirmed
    they would conduct extensive in-person and virtual meetings, going to community
    locations to ensure broad input.
- time: 02:25:56
  seconds: 8756
  topic: Data Quality from Digital Tools (George Risch)
  desc: George Risch expressed skepticism about the quality of data from digital tools
    like ThoughtExchange, preferring direct engagement. Jeff Melendez explained ThoughtExchange's
    multilingual, anonymous nature allows for broader, more candid input, and that
    it complements in-person outreach.
- time: 02:34:55
  seconds: 9295
  topic: Attracting Diverse Candidates (Eleni Richardson)
  desc: Eleni Richardson pressed on how Zeal ensures a diverse pool of national candidates,
    acknowledging the predominantly white nature of the education administration field.
    Jeff Melendez highlighted his firm's diverse team and active participation in
    conferences like 'Men of Color in Education Leadership' to proactively recruit
    diverse talent.
- time: 02:40:47
  seconds: 9647
  topic: Survey Response Goals (Dowling)
  desc: Dowling asked if Zeal uses a specific metric for survey response goals, similar
    to another firm's target of 12.5% of student enrollment. Jeff Melendez noted the
    challenge of achieving high participation but emphasized the quality of organic,
    themed feedback from ThoughtExchange over checklist-based surveys.
- time: 02:51:25
  seconds: 10285
  topic: Zeal's Weaknesses (Rosemarie DeAngelis)
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis asked Jeff Melendez to identify Zeal's weaknesses. He
    reframed the question, stating his primary concern is how to encourage boards
    and communities to invest in the complex superintendent-board relationship and
    professional governance, rather than his firm's bandwidth.
- time: 02:53:32
  seconds: 10412
  topic: Pricing and Negotiation (Eleni Richardson)
  desc: Eleni Richardson expressed 'sticker shock' at Zeal's pricing but noted the
    proposal's offer to match comparable services. She inquired about the possibility
    of negotiation, to which Jeff Melendez affirmed his willingness to discuss pricing
    to ensure value for the board.
- time: 02:55:53
  seconds: 10553
  topic: Zeal Education Group Departs
  desc: Jeff Melendez concluded his presentation and departed the meeting, allowing
    the board to begin its private deliberations.
- time: 02:56:14
  seconds: 10574
  topic: 'Board Deliberation: Initial Reactions'
  desc: The board began discussing their initial thoughts and reactions to the three
    presentations. Dowling raised the Alma proposal, which was only submitted in writing,
    noting its significantly higher cost and virtual-only approach as problematic.
- time: 03:00:35
  seconds: 10835
  topic: 'Board Deliberation: Firm Preferences and Cost Concerns'
  desc: Board members began to rank their preferences. George Risch favored NSEC for
    its detailed approach, while Claire Holman expressed strong reservations about
    Zeal's cost, suggesting MSMA's 'build your own' model might be more fiscally responsible.
    Dowling and Smith highlighted Zeal's comprehensive support and diverse team as
    valuable.
- time: 03:04:51
  seconds: 11091
  topic: 'Board Deliberation: Value of Zeal''s Approach'
  desc: Eleni Richardson and Rosemarie DeAngelis articulated their strong preference
    for Zeal, emphasizing its board-centric decision-making, diverse team, and the
    potential for the process to build board consensus. They acknowledged the cost
    but viewed it as an investment in a critical hire and board development.
- time: 03:09:12
  seconds: 11352
  topic: 'Board Deliberation: Financial Constraints'
  desc: Claire Holman reiterated concerns about the district's financial situation,
    questioning how the board could justify Zeal's higher cost when funds were tight.
    George Risch echoed this, asking how such an expense could be afforded given recent
    budget curtailments.
- time: 03:13:36
  seconds: 11616
  topic: 'Board Deliberation: Cost vs. Quality'
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis and Eleni Richardson argued that while cost was a factor,
    the quality of the superintendent search was paramount. They suggested negotiating
    with Zeal on specific cost components but stressed that compromising on core services
    would undermine the value of the investment, potentially leading to a poor hire
    that would cost more in the long run.
- time: 03:22:24
  seconds: 12144
  topic: Motion to Adjourn Workshop
  desc: George Risch moved to adjourn the workshop meeting to transition into a special
    meeting for a formal vote. Eleni Richardson seconded the motion, which passed
    unanimously.
- time: 03:22:48
  seconds: 12168
  topic: Special Meeting Roll Call
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis called the special meeting to order and conducted a roll
    call. All board members (Dowling, Feller, Holman, Richardson, Rich, Smith, DeAngelis)
    were present, while student representatives Kabisa and Davison were absent.
- time: 03:23:02
  seconds: 12182
  topic: Motion to Select Zeal Education Group
  desc: Tyler Smith made a motion to choose Zeal Education Group to assist the board
    in the superintendent search. Eleni Richardson seconded the motion, initiating
    formal discussion and voting.
- time: 03:23:33
  seconds: 12213
  topic: Discussion on Main Motion
  desc: Discussion on the motion began, with Tyler Smith affirming his previous points
    about Zeal's value, acknowledging concerns about ThoughtExchange data but emphasizing
    the board's role in weighting it. Eleni Richardson supported exploring negotiation
    on cost but highlighted the investment in board direction and diverse leadership.
- time: 03:25:56
  seconds: 12356
  topic: Cost Concerns Reiterated (Claire Holman)
  desc: Claire Holman reiterated her strong concerns about the cost, stating the board
    would be overspending and that many services could be handled internally or through
    less expensive options. She expressed skepticism about the 'bling' factor of Zeal's
    proposal versus practical needs.
- time: 03:27:30
  seconds: 12450
  topic: Financial Justification (George Risch)
  desc: George Risch questioned the financial justification for Zeal's expense, asking
    how the district could afford it given recent non-personnel spending cuts. Superintendent
    Whistle clarified that the search expense was not part of the budget curtailment,
    as it was an unanticipated need.
- time: 03:34:12
  seconds: 12852
  topic: 'Amendment to Main Motion: Reference Checks'
  desc: Claire Holman proposed an amendment to the main motion, suggesting that more
    than one person should conduct reference checks. Eleni Richardson and Rosemarie
    DeAngelis agreed to conduct these checks on all references for Zeal, and this
    amendment passed unanimously.
- time: 03:47:49
  seconds: 13669
  topic: Final Discussion on Main Motion (as amended)
  desc: The board returned to the main motion, now amended to include the reference
    check contingency. Claire Holman reiterated her belief that the district could
    achieve its goals with less expensive options, while Tyler Smith and Rosemarie
    DeAngelis emphasized the unique value of Zeal's expertise and comprehensive approach
    for the district's critical needs.
- time: 03:51:12
  seconds: 13872
  topic: Vote on Main Motion
  desc: The board voted on the main motion to select Zeal Education Group, as amended.
    The motion passed with a 5-2 vote, with Claire Holman and George Risch voting
    in opposition.
- time: 03:52:02
  seconds: 13922
  topic: Board Communications & Adjournment
  desc: Rosemarie DeAngelis confirmed the motion passed and outlined the next steps
    for conducting reference checks. Board members shared final comments, and a motion
    to adjourn the special meeting was made by Eleni Richardson and seconded by George
    Risch, passing unanimously.
